// ── Runtime (non-secret) Cloudflare Worker Variables — TEMPLATE ──────────
//
// Copy this file to `wrangler.jsonc` (gitignored) and fill in real values.
// Merged into the nitro-generated .output/server/wrangler.json on every
// `npm run build` (see node_modules/nitro/dist/_presets.mjs:
// readWranglerConfig() finds it via findNearestFile, writeWranglerConfig()
// merges it via defu(overrides, ctxConfig, userConfig, defaults) — "vars"
// is not one of nitro's own override keys, so it survives untouched into
// the deployed config).
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: these three were previously added by hand via the
// Cloudflare Dashboard as plain Variables. Because the generated wrangler
// config declared no "vars" block, `wrangler deploy` treated its own
// (var-less) config as authoritative and silently WIPED every dashboard-
// added plain Variable on each deploy — this took down Google OAuth and
// Admin access in production twice. Declaring them here instead makes them
// part of the deployed config itself, so they survive every future deploy
// automatically.
//
// Secrets (GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET, GOOGLE_OAUTH_STATE_SECRET,
// GOOGLE_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY, GMAIL_OAUTH_STATE_SECRET,
// GMAIL_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY, SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY,
// VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY) must NEVER go here — they stay as
// `wrangler secret put`-managed Secrets, which are unaffected by this bug.
{
  "vars": {
    "GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID": "your-google-oauth-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com",
    "GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI": "https://your-worker-subdomain.workers.dev/auth/google/callback",
    "ADMIN_EMAILS": "you@example.com"
  }
}
